\section{Queues}

The queue endpoints come in three authentication flavours, which is why
we look at them together: the listing, the single-queue lookup and the live
entries are public, while [[mine]] and the mutations use [[ApiAuth]].
They are a good regression test for the routing inside [[_request]].

\subsection{Listing and inspecting queues}

[[/api/queues]] and [[/api/queues/mine]] wrap their result as
[[{"queues": [...]}]] because the web client's reducer expects that shape,
whereas [[/api/queues/:queue]] returns the bare queue object.
We unwrap the list in the session so that callers always see a plain list
of queue objects ([[name]], [[locked]], [[hiding]], [[motd]], [[info]],
[[show_helper_to_assistants]]).
The unwrapping is tolerant: a server that one day returns a bare list, or
a test fixture that already does, passes through unchanged.
<<[[StayawhileSession]] methods>>=
def list_queues(self):
  """Return all public queues."""
  return unwrap_queues(
      self._request("GET", "/api/queues", use_auth=False))


def list_my_queues(self):
  """Return queues where the caller is teacher (all, for superadmins)."""
  return unwrap_queues(
      self._request("GET", "/api/queues/mine", use_api_key=True))


def get_queue(self, queue):
  """Return the queue object for `queue`."""
  return self._request("GET", self._queue_path(queue), use_auth=False)
<<helper functions>>=
def unwrap_queues(data):
  """Return the queue list from a `{"queues": [...]}` reply."""
  if isinstance(data, dict) and "queues" in data:
    return data["queues"]
  return data
