\section{Assistant presence}
\label{sec:stayawhile-presence}

Assistants announce that they are on duty by checking in, and a queue page
lists who is currently on duty.
The server keeps one open check-in row per assistant and queue, so the
endpoints differ in what they do to that row; the contrast is the whole
point of having three of them.
\begin{description}
\item[ [[POST checkin]] ] opens a session, or revives the open one.
\item[ [[POST checkout]] ] ends the session and \emph{records} it, so it
  shows up in the check-in history and the TA statistics.
\item[ [[DELETE checkin]] ] cancels: it deletes the open row \emph{without}
  recording a session, for the assistant who clicked by mistake.
\end{description}

Reading the on-duty list uses the [[Auth]] guard, so we must send the
browser JWT even though the write side accepts the API key.
We keep that asymmetry rather than ask for the key to be accepted: the
maintainer deliberately limits what scripts can do around presence, since
a TA should not be able to automate \enquote{being there}
(mail from Alexander Baltatzis, 2026-03-31).
Someone on duty is hidden from the list again after an hour of inactivity
or when the queue is unlocked.
<<[[StayawhileSession]] methods>>=
def list_on_duty(self, queue):
  """Return assistants currently on duty for `queue`."""
  return self._request("GET", self._queue_path(queue, "on_duty"))


def checkin(self, queue):
  """Check the caller in as on-duty assistant for `queue`."""
  return self._request("POST",
                       self._queue_path(queue, "checkin"),
                       use_api_key=True)


def checkout(self, queue):
  """Check the caller out of `queue`, recording the session."""
  return self._request("POST",
                       self._queue_path(queue, "checkout"),
                       use_api_key=True)


def cancel_checkin(self, queue):
  """Discard the caller's open check-in for `queue` without recording it."""
  return self._request("DELETE",
                       self._queue_path(queue, "checkin"),
                       use_api_key=True)
@

The recorded sessions can be read back, either one's own or---for
teachers---those of a named assistant.
Each session reports [[checkin_time]], [[checkout_time]],
[[last_helped_at]] and the counts [[helped]], [[presented]] and [[booked]].
Unlike the logs and statistics, these two endpoints parse [[from]] and
[[until]] as full ISO~8601 timestamps, and an unparsable value silently
falls back to the default window (the last 90~days).
To keep the CLI uniform we still accept plain dates here and widen them to
whole days ourselves; the widening is in UTC, which is what the server
compares against.

Every range query in the API---here, and in the statistics, booking and
log endpoints below---builds the same two optional parameters, so we put
that in one helper.
Skipping [[None]] values matters: passing [[until=None]] literally would
send the string [[None]] to the server, and the server rejects [[until]]
without [[from]].
<<helper functions>>=
def date_range_params(from_date=None, until=None, **extra):
  """Return query parameters for an optional `from`/`until` date range."""
  params = {}
  if from_date is not None:
    params["from"] = from_date
  if until is not None:
    params["until"] = until
  for key, value in extra.items():
    if value is not None:
      params[key] = value
  return params
@

<<[[StayawhileSession]] methods>>=
def list_my_checkins(self, queue, from_date=None, until=None):
  """Return the caller's recorded check-in sessions for `queue`."""
  return self._request("GET",
                       self._queue_path(queue, "my_checkins"),
                       params=timestamp_range_params(from_date, until),
                       use_api_key=True)


def list_ta_checkins(self, queue, username, from_date=None, until=None):
  """Return the recorded check-in sessions of `username` for `queue`."""
  suffix = f"ta_checkins/{urllib.parse.quote(username, safe='')}"
  return self._request("GET",
                       self._queue_path(queue, suffix),
                       params=timestamp_range_params(from_date, until),
                       use_api_key=True)
<<helper functions>>=
def widen_to_timestamp(value, end_of_day=False):
  """Return `value` as an ISO 8601 timestamp, widening bare dates."""
  if value is None or "T" in value:
    return value
  return f"{value}T23:59:59Z" if end_of_day else f"{value}T00:00:00Z"


def timestamp_range_params(from_date=None, until=None):
  """Return `from`/`until` query parameters as full timestamps."""
  return date_range_params(widen_to_timestamp(from_date),
                           widen_to_timestamp(until, end_of_day=True))
