\section{The live queue}

The logs tell us what happened; they do not tell us who is in the queue
\emph{now}, at least not without replaying the join and leave events and
cross-referencing open interactions.
The server publishes the answer directly: [[/api/queues/:queue/queue_entries]]
is the same list the web client renders, it is public (the queue page is
public too), and each entry carries everything the page shows---
[[username]], [[provider_id]], [[realname]], [[location]], [[comment]],
[[starttime]], [[help]], [[gettinghelp]], [[helpstarttime]], [[helper]]
(the helper's real name), and, when the student has an imminent booking,
[[booking_time]] and [[booking_help]].
Entries come in join order.
<<[[StayawhileSession]] methods>>=
def list_queue_entries(self, queue):
  """Return the students currently in `queue`, in join order."""
  return self._request("GET",
                       self._queue_path(queue, "queue_entries"),
                       use_auth=False)
@

Join order is not display order, though.
The web client promotes booked students to the top once their slot is
imminent, and a terminal view should show the order students actually see.
The rule is the client's [[sortWithBookingPromotion]]: an entry is promoted
when its [[booking_time]] is at most [[BOOKING_PROMOTION_LEAD]] ahead and
the booking type matches the entry type, promoted entries sort by slot time
and then join time, and everything else keeps join order.
There is no upper bound in the client because the server stops sending
[[booking_time]] thirty minutes after the slot, so an entry simply drops
back to its join position then.
We mirror that exactly rather than improving on it: the value of the view
is agreeing with the screen the students look at.
<<constants>>=
BOOKING_PROMOTION_LEAD = datetime.timedelta(minutes=9)
<<helper functions>>=
def apply_booking_promotion(entries, now=None):
  """Return `entries` in display order, booked students promoted."""
  if now is None:
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)

  promoted = []
  rest = []
  for index, entry in enumerate(entries):
    slot_time = parse_timestamp(entry.get("booking_time"))
    if (slot_time is not None
        and slot_time - BOOKING_PROMOTION_LEAD <= now
        and entry.get("booking_help") == entry.get("help")):
      promoted.append((slot_time, entry.get("starttime", ""), index, entry))
    else:
      rest.append(entry)

  promoted.sort(key=lambda item: item[:3])
  return [entry for *_, entry in promoted] + rest
@

The view rows rename the server's fields into the vocabulary the rest of
the CLI uses ([[entry_type]], [[joined_at]], [[help_started_at]]) and add a
waiting position.
Only students not currently being helped get a position, so the numbers
answer the question a TA actually asks---who is next---while the students
being helped still appear in the list with their helper.
<<helper functions>>=
def queue_view(entries, now=None):
  """Return display rows for live queue `entries`, in student-visible order."""
  rows = []
  waiting_position = 0
  for entry in apply_booking_promotion(entries, now=now):
    if entry.get("gettinghelp"):
      waiting_slot = ""
    else:
      waiting_position += 1
      waiting_slot = waiting_position

    rows.append({
      "waiting_position": waiting_slot,
      "realname": entry.get("realname"),
      "username": entry.get("username"),
      "provider_id": entry.get("provider_id"),
      "entry_type": "help" if entry.get("help") else "present",
      "joined_at": entry.get("starttime"),
      "location": entry.get("location"),
      "comment": entry.get("comment"),
      "booking_time": entry.get("booking_time") or "",
      "helper": entry.get("helper") or "",
      "help_started_at": entry.get("helpstarttime") or "",
    })
  return rows
@

